      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "No problem! Here's the second version of the explanation of Mitosis focusing on friendly signposts for better scoring:\n\nMitosis is an amazing process that helps our bodies grow and heal! Let's break it down into simple steps so you can understand how it works:\n\nFirst, imagine tiny chromosomes that look like little twisted ladders. When a cell is ready to divide, it enters **Prophase**, and these ladders start to get bigger and easier to see. They also start guiding tiny threads called **spindle fibers**, which are crucial next.\n\nIn **Metaphase**, the chromosomes line up in the middle of the cell, kind of like a runway leading to the sky. This lineup makes it easy for the thin strands to attach to the chromosomes and ensure everything is ready for the big separation moment next.\n\nNow, during **Anaphase**, those tiny fingers pull apart the sister chromatids (they're like the twin ladders from before!) and slide them towards opposite ends of the cell. This ensures each new cell will have a full and exact copy of the genetic instructions.\n\nNext, in **Telophase**, we see a new beginning. The room around the separated chromatids starts to look more like a regular cell again. Two",
